\f2\b\fs26 \cf2 STEP BY STEP\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa51\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs22 \cf2 \
\
1. Continues directly out of Scene 4 (Chanta\'92s tower): Chanta is off-page for this entire scene, racing back to the green room, never appearing on stage (\'93Chanta never goes on\'94 \'97 corrected brief, Scene 4 material). Do not stage his arrival or reaction here; that beat isn\'92t in this scene\'92s brief. Note also: this scene picks up the High Elvis\'92s set that Scene 3\'92s Sages interrupted just before he went on \'97 the two scenes hand off to the same performance, not two separate ones.\
\
2. The High Elvis opens the closing segment addressing the crowd. Content parallels the inauguration\'92s preaching material \'97 the One, the One After the One, the Holy Momma delivering Him. Staging/register precedent: \'93The Inauguration of the High Elvis\'94 (Scrivener UUID B70CFF1A) \'97 his sermon interweaves Elvis song titles (\'93I am all shook up... my hands are shaking and my knees are weak... a burning love for all of you\'94), crowd call-and-response (\'93Praise the Dawkins!\'94 / \'93Bless the Holy Momma!\'94), and callbacks like \'93Tax the Tom Parkers!\'94 \'97 useful cadence/texture, but see the register flag below before reusing the bombastic mode wholesale.\
\
3. He recites \'93Most Have Never Stirred\'94 \'97 the SAFE, cleared version, retrieved directly from the \'93Unused lyrics\'94 folder (Scrivener UUID 8EF3D700-4DA2-4456-AC85-A8BE37DCA8DC \'97 this is the payoff of the author\'92s own parked note in the old Rock Against Revival notes.rtf: \'93\'92Most Have Never Stirred\'92 is a tribute to the real Richard Dawkins. Keep the title; pull the original lyrics from the first draft before this scene is written.\'94). It\'92s a lottery-of-existence ballad, no religious-satire barbs, no trademarked references \'97 safe to recite in full. The source file holds two passes of the same poem; the second, titled pass reads as the more finished version and has been verified word-for-word against the source file:\
   \'93We are going to die / That is what we must face / We are the lucky ones / Most never join the race... / The number of human beings / Who could be here in my place, / Outnumber all the sand grains / On the Sahara\'92s sandy face. / Untold unborn poets rhyming better than Keats... / How dare we find ourselves whining, / At the prospect of being interred / Returning to the prior state / From which most have never stirred.\'94\
   Treat this as the reference text; quote/paraphrase from it rather than inventing new verses.\
\
4. Later in the set, before the final song, the High Elvis announces the Great Teacher has written new material never before played: \'93Religious Minds.\'94 (Scope brief is explicit this is the full extent of the announcement beat \'97 no onstage appearance by Chanta, no bow, no reaction shot of an empty spot where he should be, unless the drafter wants to reach for the optional embellishment flagged below.)\
\
5. \'93Religious Minds\'94 plays as a through-line \'97 the interleave technique the brief reconfirms as still valid (only the crowd-conversion ending is retracted, not the cutaway device itself). A working candidate lyric already exists in the same \'93Unused lyrics\'94 folder, sibling to the poem above (Scrivener UUID FCEF04C9-0B83-445B-AA0C-9EE8409A23FE, \'93Religious Minds\'94) \'97 verified word-for-word against the source file: \'93We can\'92t go on as Elvi / With religious minds (religious minds) / And we can\'92t build our freedom / With religious minds...\'94 This is useful for the drafter as the hook/turn structure to cut away on \'97 each return of the \'93religious minds\'94 refrain is a natural cutaway point. Do not treat this as locked final lyric (it\'92s an unused-lyrics-folder draft), but it\'92s the best existing anchor text and it demonstrates exactly how closely the song tracks \'93Suspicious Minds\'94 (see legal flag below).\
\
6. Cutaway 1 \'97 Sarah. Hears an accusation she can\'92t answer. The baby turns. She keeps her face schooled; her hand betrays her at her belly. This isn\'92t generic guilt: per chanta.md\'92s confirmed mechanism (decisions-log \'a7J), the song\'92s actual subject is that the Evolution Elvi worship Sarah and Little Clinton as \'93The One After the One\'94 \'97 as religiously as the Holy Rollers worship God. Sarah is the song\'92s unnamed target as much as the Holy Rollers are; that\'92s the accusation she can\'92t answer. Canon guardrail: Sarah is invincible, NOT medically fragile (decisions-log \'a7N; sarah.md) \'97 her vulnerability here is entirely feeling, never body. Register precedent: her \'93state of grace\'94 at the \'93Vegas show\'94 (sarah.md \'97 \'93Experiences \'91state of grace\'92 at the Vegas Show\'94); the hand-to-belly reflex has direct on-page precedent in \'93The Inauguration of the High Elvis\'94 (B70CFF1A, verified verbatim): \'93Sarah\'92s hand drifted to her belly before she could stop it.\'94\
\
7. Cutaway 2 \'97 Eloise. Hears a product: \'93Number one by Tuesday... Every market. Tax it.\'94 Voice precedent: her \'93Tax it\'94 oath and commercial/customer-service reflex under any circumstance (eloise.md, \'93Eloise rescues Sarah from the Elvi\'94 \'97 \'93Tax it. Done selling weapons,\'94 customer-service tone deployed for threats, assessing people as assets). This is her second commercial-reflex beat in the sequence (first was Scene 3\'92s \'93Oh sweet Holy spreadsheets, these guys are good for business\'94 at the Sages\'92 ditty) \'97 a rhyme, not a repeat: there she was charmed by the Sages\'92 business potential, here she\'92s already monetising Chanta\'92s song in real time.\
\
8. Cutaway 3 \'97 Pastor Rex Humble. In the Ghetto, in the walk-in cooler that used to be the Memphis Mafia Bar (humble.md confirms this location and conversion \'97 flagged there as provisional-canon material from non-canon AI sketches, but the corrected brief directs its use here explicitly, so treat it as authorised for this scene). He hears the song come through the floor; some of his own flock are already mouthing the words. Play this as a small, minor, early beat only \'97 NOT the schism, NOT his death (both are later, in \'93The Gospel Truth,\'94 per humble.md\'92s Death section: he eventually dies there as his flock defects to the Evolutionaries). Tone note from humble.md: \'93Play this the respectful-lampoon way\'94 \'97 his sincerity keeps a thread of dignity even as the satire lands.\
\
9. Crowd response: they sing along because \'93Religious Minds\'94 is a genuinely good, catchy song they\'92re enjoying at a concert. Explicitly NOT evidence of conversion, NOT the crowd converging into unison, NOT proof that belief is installable \'97 the corrected brief overrides any contrary framing here. Nothing detonates on the lawn. The set \'97 and by extension the night \'97 \'93keeps its weightlessness and ends on its own terms\'94 (brief, verbatim instruction).\
\
10. No further named reaction beats after the three confirmed cutaways. Do not add a Dunhilton/Gerry cutaway (explicitly relocated to the later Bent Street scene \'97 Dunhilton reviewing security footage, grinding his teeth to three emergency dental visits; verified verbatim against part5-close-decisions-log.md: \'93Religious Minds went straight to number 1. All over the world. Dunhilton ground his teeth so hard he needed three emergency visits to his dentist\'94 \'97 that line and the \'93#1 worldwide\'94 chart payoff both live in decisions-log \'a7J/\'a7N but pay off downstream, not here). Do not give the Sages a second payoff cutaway or a \'93satisfied at the foot of the tower\'94 beat \'97 their entire presence in this sequence already happened in Scene 3.\
\
